 "sp-shasta-route-roseville-hornbrook", "color": "#C1272D", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 35, "speed_source": "estimated typical steam/early-diesel-era mainline speed for a mountainous route; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1887, "end": 1924, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Construction resumed in the 1880s, pushing north from Redding through Dunsmuir and over the Siskiyou Summit (4,133 ft, an 18-tunnel crossing). On Dec 17 1887, Southern Pacific president Charles Crocker drove the last spike at Ashland, joining this line with the Oregon and California Railroad building south from Portland -- the first-ever Sacramento/San Francisco-Portland rail connection, marketed as the Shasta Route. The Oregon & California Express (later briefly the Shasta Limited) ran this route until November 1924.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shasta_Route", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redding", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siskiyou_Line"]}, {"start": 1924, "end": 1926, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Some secondary or local passenger service likely continued after the Express's 1924 discontinuation, though a specific successor train is not documented here; treated as reduced/local-only service pending better sourcing (matches the corresponding era in oc_railroad.geojson).", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shasta_Route", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redding", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siskiyou_Line"]}, {"start": 1926, "end": 1958, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Southern Pacific completed the lower-grade Cascade Line via Klamath Falls in 1926 (see southern_pacific_cascade.geojson), which took over as the primary Portland-San Francisco route; the steep, curvy Siskiyou Line settled into secondary status. A specific end date for through passenger service on this exact routing isn't well documented; 1958 is used here as an approximate placeholder, matching oc_railroad.geojson, for the general national decline of secondary rail passenger service in the late 1950s.", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shasta_Route", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redding", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siskiyou_Line"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-121.6198645, 39.1647619], "to": [-121.819902, 39.6986385], "chord_mi": 38.39, "traced_mi": 38.42, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Valley Subdivision (38.42 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-121.9562362, 39.7790118], "to": [-122.0472358, 39.9179549], "chord_mi": 10.75, "traced_mi": 10.75, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Valley Subdivision (10.75 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-121.6198645, 39.1647619], "to": [-121.819902, 39.6986385], "chord_mi": 38.39, "traced_mi": 38.42, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Valley Subdivision (38.42 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-121.9562362, 39.7790118], "to": [-122.0472358, 39.9179549], "chord_mi": 10.75, "traced_mi": 10.75, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Valley Subdivision (10.75 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 38 mi replaced with Ope